  "line": 11,
  "situation": "Four of the six titles are the Vulgate's own word, lifted from the verse printed beside them. Tit. ii. 14 *ut mundaret sibi populum* gives `mundator`; Heb. ii. 15 *ut liberaret eos* gives `liberator`; Col. ii. 15 *palam triumphans illos* gives `triumphator`; Heb. vi. 20 *ubi præcursor … introivit Iesus* gives `præcursor`, the only place the word occurs in the New Testament. The catalogue is quarried, not composed. Exactly two depart, and both depart somewhere. Rom. viii. 34 says only *interpellat pro nobis*; `Advocatus` is at 1 John ii. 1. And Heb. xii. 2 reads *auctorem fidei et consummatorem* where the plate sets `Instaurator` — the verb of Eph. i. 10, *instaurare omnia in Christo*. At the second Advent the title is not the finisher of our faith but the restorer of all things; the English keeps \"finisher\" because the reference is to Hebrews and Part III renders it so.",
  "source": "identified",
  "band": "R",
  "numbering": true
